{
 "title": "Roshan Shaam (The Glowing Evening)",
 "concept": "The mukhda is a lilting 12/8 question and answer: a three-note pickup (A-B-D) opens into a long F# reached by meend, climbs to A, sighs down through the Khamaj komal Ni (D-C-B), then rises to a B5 peak and resolves C#-D. It grows in stages. The bansuri breathes its outline over santoor shimmer; the solo violin introduces the first phrase in the garden; groom and bride get mirror phrases landing on their names (flute B5 at 11.0, violin C#6 at 17.0). With the couple the full mukhda arrives as a violin-bansuri dialogue joined in thirds; at the stage the strings sweep it in octaves under a bansuri descant; the card holds the delayed cadence and a hushed B-minor reflection; a G-A-B7 lift modulates up a whole step so the bansuri finally sings the theme in E major, ending on a long E with a murki.",
 "meter": "12/8",
 "key": "D major with Khamaj colour; modulates up a whole step to E major for the finale (bar 17, 51.0 s) via G - A - B7sus4 - B7",
 "mode_notes": "Raga Khamaj colour inside a Bollywood major: shuddha Ni (C#) in ascents and cadences, komal Ni (C natural) only in descending phrases - the signature meend D5-C5-B4 of T2 - supported by the bVII chord C(add9) in bars 8 and 11. Warmth from the relative minor area (Bm9, F#m7, Em9) rather than sadness; sus4 chords resolve by step (A7sus4 to A7 at 8.25 s, B7sus4 to B7 at 56.25 s). No tivra Ma. The finale in E major keeps the same grammar: D# (shuddha Ni) rising in the pickup B-C#-D#, and the closing murki F#-E-D#-E. Ornaments: ~ meend on almost every long note, ^ kan on phrase turns.",
